 ## Background
-The concept of Style Transfer Functions for Illustrative Volume Rendering was introduced by Stefan Bruckner and Marcus E. Gröller in 2007 [S1]. The method was developed to address the limitations of photorealistic rendering in conveying structural information, drawing inspiration from technical illustrations and the "100-percent-rule" where transparency falls off near edges [S1]. It was implemented using GPU-based ray casting with Shader Model 3.0, integrating seamlessly with existing transfer function pipelines by replacing the color output of the 1D texture lookup with a style index [S1].
+The concept of Style Transfer Functions for Illustrative Volume Rendering was introduced by Stefan Bruckner and Eduard Gröller in 2007 [S1]. The method was implemented in C++ and OpenGL/GLSL, leveraging Shader Model 3.0 features such as conditional loops and dynamic branching [S1]. It allows for the integration of illustrative techniques, such as contour lines and variable transparency, into existing GPU-based ray casting renderers with minimal effort [S1].
 
